<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maximilian Henry of Bavaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1621">1621</a>–<a href="/years/1688">1688</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maximilian Henry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> was archbishop-elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> from 1650 and prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Liège and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a>. He allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and in Liège his Regulation of 1684 abolished the political power of the craft <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> and placed the city under princely control.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maximilian-henry-of-bavaria.jpg" alt="Maximilian Henry of Bavaria" width="320" height="467" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lambert-van-den-Bos-Lieuwe-van-Aitzema-Historien-onses-tyds_MGG_0400.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
